# elohim/ — the protocol substrate (interface-first reuse)

This is the root of the elohim protocol substrate: **eprfs** (content-addressing — `BlobCid`,
canonical agents, meta), **epr** (the canonical dag-cbor codec + `compute_cid`, the identity-minting
envelope), **elohim-storage** (the HTTP/DB truth layer), the **holochain** DNAs, **elohim-facings**,
and **sdk/schemas** (the wire-shape source of truth). Identity math, the codec, and the wire shapes
are minted here **once**; everything else consumes them.

Vendored git submodules under this tree — `brit`, `rakia`, `rust-ipfs`, `tx5`, `kitsune2`,
`holochain-conductor` — carry their own `.git` and terminate the ancestor walk, so this manifest does
**not** govern inside them (brit governs itself on `brit-dev`). That boundary is exactly why the
BritCid↔BlobCid drift below could happen across the seam without either side's gate catching it.

## interface-first-reuse (inject — advise, never block)

Before a developer or agent authors a bespoke method that **re-implements an existing canonical
interface**, the gate surfaces the interface. The recurrence this prevents is the duplication class
found in the 2026-07-12 shared-crate audit
(`brit/docs/specs/2026-07-12-shared-crate-consolidation-design.md` §1): `BritCid` re-implementing
`eprfs-core::BlobCid`, `drift_fingerprint` re-deriving the short-form fingerprint, and two `Verdict`
enums — each re-derived instead of shared, then drifting.

Four rules cover the code surfaces (`*.rs`, `*.ts`, `*.mjs`, `*.py`); each fires only when the
written content contains a re-implementation smell (a CID/codec/fingerprint/verdict symbol via
`contains-any`), so ordinary edits are never touched. The signal is **content-shaped**, not
placement-shaped, and each rule stands on its class ladder plus a `dedupe-of` pointer at the
canonical home it is protecting.

The rules name **no validator**, and the phantom `epr:validator-interface-first-reuse` ref they
used to carry was dropped on 2026-08-02 (plan task P4.1). The reason is the discipline itself:
"is this a re-implementation of an existing interface?" is a semantic judgement, not a keyword
match, so no honest predicate exists yet — and a `validator:` key naming a detector the resolver
does not implement advertises enforcement the rule does not serve (concern class C7, the class
whose own type specimen was 15 policy rules naming validators that do not exist). The advisory is
unchanged in class, trigger and text; only the false advertisement is gone. When a real semantic
detector is written, it arrives as a registered `epr:validator-*` and these rules graduate then —
the `why:` already carries the destination.

The canonical interfaces the `why:` routes to:

| Re-implementation smell | Canonical home |
|---|---|
| CID compute / compute_raw / short-form fingerprint | `eprfs-core::BlobCid` — `elohim/eprfs/eprfs-core/src/address.rs` |
| dag-cbor codec + `compute_cid` (the minting envelope) | `elohim/epr` — `src/cid.rs`, `src/cbor.rs`, `src/envelope.rs` |
| cite short-form (`sha256:<16-hex>` of the body CID) | `.claude/scripts/_lib/cite_graph.py` (decode-only oracle) |
| wire shapes / view types | `elohim/sdk/schemas` (generated to TS via codegen) |

**Consumer surfaces beyond this tree** (`doorway/`, `steward/`) also compute blob CIDs and would carry
the same concern; the proper multi-directory home for one shared rule is a policy binding
(`.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`, `policy: <id>@<version>`), not a sprayed inline copy. Lifting this to
a policy is deferred — this manifest governs the tree where the audited drift actually lives and where
the canonical interfaces are minted.
